i":1},{"n":"Masoutis","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":39.625318,"lo":19.919063,"d":831,"h":"Mo-Fr 08:00-21:00; Sa 08:00-20:00; Su off"},{"n":"ΧΡΙΣΤΙΝΑ ΠΑΝΔΗ ΚΑΙ ΣΙΑ ΟΕ","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":39.625332,"lo":19.919487,"d":864,"h":"May 01-Sep 15: Mo-Tu 08:30-14:00,18:00-21:00; We 08:30-14:00; Th-Fr 08:30-14:00,18:00-21:00; Sep 16-Apr 30: Mo-Tu 08:30-14:00,17:30-20:30; We 08:30-14:00; Th-Fr 08:30-14:00,17:30-20:30","ph":"+302661 039281"},{"n":"ΚΟΥΡΗ ΜΑΡΙΝΑ","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":39.626,"lo":19.919889,"d":875,"h":"May 01-Sep 15: Mo-Tu 08:30-14:00,18:00-21:00; We 08:30-14:00; Th-Fr 08:30-14:00,18:00-21:00; Sep 16-Apr 30: Mo-Tu 08:30-14:00,17:30-20:30; We 08:30-14:00; Th-Fr 08:30-14:00,17:30-20:30","ph":"+302661 045306"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":39.624487,"lo":19.919483,"d":901},{"n":"Piraeus Bank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":39.62127,"lo":19.904091,"d":903},{"n":"Dimitra","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":39.620963,"lo":19.90464,"d":907},{"n":"Platytera Monastery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.622008,"lo":19.910408,"d":667,"ds":"The Platytera Monastery (Greek: Μονή Πλατυτέρας), formally the Holy Monastery of the Most Holy Mother of God Platytera (Ιερά Μονή Υπεραγίας Θεοτόκου Πλατυτέρας), is a Greek Orthodox monastery in the city of Corfu in Greece. It was founded in 1741 and finished in 1743, during the last period of Venetian rule. It was then rebuilt after being almost destroyed in the Siege of Corfu (1798–1799).","wpu":"Platytera Monastery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Platiterna_courtyard.jpg/960px-Platiterna_courtyard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palace of St. Michael and St. George","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.625833,"lo":19.924694,"d":1281,"ds":"The Palace of St. Michael and St. George (Greek: Ανάκτορο των Αγίων Μιχαήλ και Γεωργίου) is a palace in Corfu City on the island of Corfu, Greece.","wpu":"Palace of St. Michael and St. George","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Palau_de_sant_Miquel_i_sant_Jordi_-_Palaia_Anaktora%2C_Corf%C3%BA.JPG/960px-Palau_de_sant_Miquel_i_sant_Jordi_-_Palaia_Anaktora%2C_Corf%C3%BA.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Spianada","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.623333,"lo":19.924444,"d":1341,"ds":"Spianada is a large central square located in the city of Corfu, Greece. It is the largest square in Greece, the biggest in Europe and the 27th biggest in world by area and is situated directly in front of the Old Fortress.","wpu":"Spianada","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/2001-09_Corfu-Stadt-14.jpg/960px-2001-09_Corfu-Stadt-14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Maitland Monument","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.621722,"lo":19.924528,"d":1426,"ds":"The Maitland Monument, also known as the Maitland Rotunda or the Peristyle of Maitland (Greek: Περιστύλιο του Μαίτλαντ), is a neoclassical monument located at the end of Spianada Square in Corfu. It was built in 1821 to honour Sir Thomas Maitland, a British military officer who was the last Civil Commissioner and first Lord High Commissioner of the Ionian Islands.","wpu":"Maitland Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Maitland_Monument_in_Corfu_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Maitland_Monument_in_Corfu_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Archaeological Museum of Corfu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.6188,"lo":19.9219,"d":1444,"ds":"The Archaeological Museum of Corfu (Greek: Αρχαιολογικό Μουσείο Κέρκυρας) in Corfu, Greece was built between 1962 and 1965. The museum land was donated by the city of Corfu. Its initial purpose was to house the archaeological finds from the Temple of Artemis in Corfu.","wpu":"Archaeological Museum of Corfu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Archaeological_Museum_of_Corfu.jpg/960px-Archaeological_Museum_of_Corfu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Tomb of Menecrates","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.616806,"lo":19.919333,"d":1479,"ds":"The Tomb of Menecrates or Monument of Menecrates is an Archaic-period cenotaph in Corfu, Greece, built around 600 BC in the ancient city of Korkyra (or Corcyra). The tomb and the funerary sculpture of a lion were discovered in 1843 during demolition works by the British army in the United States of the Ionian Islands who were demolishing a Venetian-era fortress in the site of Garitsa hill in Corfu.","wpu":"Tomb of Menecrates","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Tomb_of_Menekrates_in_Corfu.jpg/960px-Tomb_of_Menekrates_in_Corfu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Fortress, Corfu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.623,"lo":19.93,"d":1800,"ds":"The Old Fortress of Corfu (Greek: Παλαιό Φρούριο, Venetian: Fortezza Vecchia) is a Venetian fortress in the city of Corfu, Greece. The fortress covers the promontory which initially contained the old town of Corfu that had emerged during Byzantine times.","wpu":"Old Fortress, Corfu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Kerkyrapalaiofrourio.JPG/960px-Kerkyrapalaiofrourio.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Corcyra (polis)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.607222,"lo":19.918333,"d":2418,"ds":"Corcyra (also Korkyra ; Ancient Greek: Κόρκυρα) was an ancient Greek city on the island of Corfu in the Ionian Sea adjacent to Epirus. It was a colony of Corinth founded in the Archaic period. Corcyra acted as a port of call for ships sailing west, especially to the Italian coast or farther north.","wpu":"Corcyra (polis)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Gorgon_at_the_Archaeological_Museum_in_Corfu.jpg/960px-Gorgon_at_the_Archaeological_Museum_in_Corfu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mon Repos, Corfu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.606389,"lo":19.926111,"d":2771,"ds":"Mon Repos (French: [mɔ̃ ʁ(ə)po]) is a former royal summer residence on the island of Corfu, Greece. It lies south of Corfu City in the forest of Palaeopolis. Since 2001, it has housed the Museum of Palaiopolis—Mon Repos.","wpu":"Mon Repos, Corfu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Corfu_Mon_Repos_R01.jpg/960px-Corfu_Mon_Repos_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kardaki Temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.601389,"lo":19.926111,"d":3265,"ds":"Kardaki Temple is an Archaic Doric temple in Corfu, Greece, built around 500 BC in the ancient city of Korkyra (or Corcyra), in what is known today as the location Kardaki in the hill of Analipsi in Corfu. The temple features several architectural peculiarities that point to a Doric origin. The temple at Kardaki is unusual because it has no frieze, following perhaps architectural tendencies of Sicilian temples.","wpu":"Kardaki Temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Kardaki_Temple_south_side.jpg/960px-Kardaki_Temple_south_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lazaretto Island","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.648889,"lo":19.880556,"d":3428,"ds":"Lazaretto Island, (Greek: Λαζαρέτο, also Lazareto, formerly known as Aghios Dimitrios) is located two nautical miles northeast of the city of Corfu. The island has an area of 7 ha (17 acres) and is administered by the Greek National Tourist Organization. It gets its name from the lazaretto located there.","wpu":"Lazaretto Island","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Lazaretto_island_%28Corfu%29.jpg/960px-Lazaretto_island_%28Corfu%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pontikonisi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.586389,"lo":19.917778,"d":4674,"ds":"Pontikonisi (Greek: Ποντικονήσι, \"Mouse Island\") is a Greek islet near the island of Corfu. Its prominent feature is a Byzantine chapel of Christ Pantokrator, dating from the 11th or 12th century. The island of Pontikonisi might have served as an inspiration for Arnold Böcklin's painting Isle of the Dead.","wpu":"Pontikonisi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Pontikonisi_Island.jpg/960px-Pontikonisi_Island.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Venetian arsenal, Gouvia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.652461,"lo":19.846844,"d":6053,"ds":"The Venetian arsenal at Gouvia was a shipyard built by the Republic of Venice during their rule over the island of Corfu. It was located on the west side of what used to be called \"Govino Bay\", the current location of the modern village of Gouvia. The arsenal was built in 1716 as part of defenses against the Ottomans.","wpu":"Venetian arsenal, Gouvia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Venetian_Shipyard_in_Gouvia%2C_Corfu.jpg/960px-Venetian_Shipyard_in_Gouvia%2C_Corfu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Achilleio","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.566667,"lo":19.9,"d":6873,"ds":"Achilleio (Greek: Αχίλλειο) is a former municipality on the island of Corfu, Ionian Islands, Greece. Since the 2019 local government reform it is part of the municipality Central Corfu and Diapontia Islands, of which it is a municipal unit. It is located in the south-central part of the island of Corfu, south of Corfu (city).","wpu":"Achilleio","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Corfu_Achilleion_R05.jpg/960px-Corfu_Achilleion_R05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Achilleion (Corfu)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.5625,"lo":19.904167,"d":7300,"ds":"The Achilleion (Greek: Αχίλλειο, Katharevousa: Αχίλλειον) is a palace built on Corfu for Empress Elisabeth of Austria after a suggestion by the Austrian consul Alexander von Warsberg. Elisabeth was deeply saddened by the tragic loss of her only son, Crown Prince Rudolf of Austria following the Mayerling incident in 1889, and a year later she had this summer palace built as a refuge.","wpu":"Achilleion (Corfu)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Corfu_Achilleion_R05.jpg/960px-Corfu_Achilleion_R05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sinarades","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.566667,"lo":19.85,"d":8540,"ds":"Sinarades (Greek: Σιναράδες) is a town and a community in the northern part of the island of Corfu, Greece, part of the municipal unit of Parelioi. The community includes the small villages Aspes and Kontogialos. Sinarades is the largest village of Parelioi.","wpu":"Sinarades","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Corfu_Sinarades_R01.jpg/960px-Corfu_Sinarades_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Parelioi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.6,"lo":19.816667,"d":8579,"ds":"Parelioi (Greek: Παρέλιοι) is a former municipality on the island of Corfu, Ionian Islands, Greece. Since the 2019 local government reform it is part of the municipality Central Corfu and Diapontia Islands, of which it is a municipal unit. It is located on the central west coast of the island of Corfu.","wpu":"Parelioi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Giannades_Parelia_Faiakes_12_01_02_970000.jpeg/960px-Giannades_Parelia_Faiakes_12_01_02_970000.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stillo Islet","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.685278,"lo":19.989722,"d":9334,"ds":"Stillo Islet (Albanian: Ishulli i Stillos) or Stil Islet is a small islet located in Xarrë, southern Albania, on the Ionian Sea.","wpu":"Stillo Islet","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Stillo_Island_Albania.jpg/960px-Stillo_Island_Albania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kato Korakiana","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.689389,"lo":19.831694,"d":9567,"ds":"Kato Korakiana (Greek: Κάτω Κορακιάνα) is a Greek village on the island of Corfu.","wpu":"Kato Korakiana","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CE%AE_%CE%A0%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B1%CE%BA%CE%BF%CE%B8%CE%AE%CE%BA%CE%B7-%CE%9C%CE%BF%CF%85%CF%83%CE%B5%CE%AF%CE%BF_%CE%91%CE%BB%CE%B5%CE%BE%CE%AC%CE%BD%CE%B4%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%A3%CE%BF%CF%8D%CF%84%CE%B6%CE%BF%CF%85%2C_%CE%A0%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%81%CF%84%CE%B7%CE%BC%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%AD%CF%81%CE%BA%CF%85%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ipsos, Corfu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.694722,"lo":19.836667,"d":9718,"ds":"Ipsos, also written as Ypsos (Greek: Ύψος), is a Greek village on the island of Corfu. It is the seat of the Faiakes community in the municipality of Central Corfu and Diapontian Islands.","wpu":"Ipsos, Corfu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Ypsos_2017_02.jpg/960px-Ypsos_2017_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Corfu ( kor-FOO, -⁠FEW, US also KOR-foo, -⁠few) or Kerkyra is one of the Ionian Islands in western Greece, and the northernmost island on Greece's west coast except for its satellite Diapontian Islands, which are also the westernmost point of all Greece. Corfu and the Diapontian Islands mark the International Hydrographic Organization border between the Ionian Sea to the south and the Adriatic Sea to the north. Within the Ionian Islands region, the regional unit of Corfu extends as far south as the Paxoi.","wpu":"Corfu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Pontikonisi.jpg/960px-Pontikonisi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}